{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2017",
    "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "esnext"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "jsx": "preserve",
    "incremental": true,

    /*
     * Both of the options below are required by TypeScript 6 and are inert on 5.
     *
     * `types` pins which ambient `@types/*` packages enter the program.
     * TypeScript 5 loaded every package in `node_modules/@types` — 31 of them
     * here — while TypeScript 6 loads only the ones reachable through a real
     * `import`. `@types/jest` is reachable no other way: it declares
     * `describe`, `it`, `expect` and the `jest` namespace as globals, and not
     * one of this repository's 54 test files imports them from
     * `@jest/globals`. Without this line the upgrade produces 3148 errors, all
     * of them in test files. Only `jest` and `node` need naming; the other ten
     * `@types/*` dependencies are module typings that come in through their own
     * imports and are unaffected.
     *
     * `rootDir` is set because TypeScript 6 refuses to infer it: it now errors
     * with TS5011 when the common source directory of the program is
     * ambiguous. `ts-jest` compiles one file at a time, so the "common source
     * directory" of each compilation is whatever folder that single file lives
     * in — `./src/utils`, `./scripts`, and so on — and 53 of 54 suites fail to
     * *load*. That failure mode is the dangerous one, and the one SCRUM-324 was
     * filed for: the suites never run, so `yarn test` reports the tests that
     * remain as passing rather than reporting a failure. `tsc` itself is
     * unaffected either way, because `noEmit` means no output layout is ever
     * produced.
     */
    "types": ["jest", "node"],
    "rootDir": ".",

    /*
     * An unused import used to pass every check this project runs, and that is
     * not merely untidy — a dead import is a false statement about what a file
     * depends on. `AccountSection.tsx` imported `handleMonthChange` and stopped
     * calling it in February 2025, which made the file read as though the
     * profile had adopted the UTC date fix. It had not. SCRUM-381 was written
     * on that assumption and got its analysis wrong; SCRUM-393, the live data
     * bug behind it, stayed invisible for eighteen months. SCRUM-394.
     *
     * `noUnusedLocals` here rather than `@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars`,
     * reversing what SCRUM-394 first proposed, for two reasons:
     *
     *   - **Coverage.** `yarn lint` globs `src/**` only, so ESLint never sees
     *     `prisma/` or `scripts/`. Four of the 59 findings cleared alongside
     *     this were in `prisma/seed.ts`, including an unused *function*. This
     *     `include` covers every compiled file.
     *   - **No new plugin.** The rule needs `@typescript-eslint` registered in
     *     `.eslintrc.json`; this needs nothing, and `yarn tsc` is already a CI
     *     job.
     *
     * `noUnusedParameters` is deliberately **off**. It is a different question:
     * an unused parameter is usually a signature the caller dictates - a React
     * prop, an event handler's second argument, `clearMarkers(_map)` in
     * `viewRoute.ts` - and forcing every one of those to be renamed or deleted
     * would trade a real check for a stream of noise. `noUnusedLocals` does not
     * look at parameters at all, which is why no `^_` escape hatch is needed.
     */
    "noUnusedLocals": true
  },
  "include": [
    "next-env.d.ts",
    "next-auth.d.ts",
    "**/*.d.ts",
    "**/*.ts",
    "**/*.tsx"
  ],
  "exclude": ["node_modules"],
  "ts-node": {
    "compilerOptions": {
      "module": "commonjs"
    }
  }
}
